          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Two Variables, One Line"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.841,
            0.07,
            0.881,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "107"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.304,
            0.11,
            0.62,
            0.15
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_22_equation_2.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{array} { r l } { \\mathrm { o r } \\quad } & { { } \\quad 2 1 - 1 4 y - 1 5 y = 2 } \\\\ { \\mathrm { o r } \\quad } & { { } \\quad - 2 9 y = - 1 9 } \\end{array}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.156,
            0.353,
            0.192
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Therefore, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y = { \\frac { 1 9 } { 2 9 } }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.199,
            0.881,
            0.239
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Step 3:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " We can find the value of x by substituting the value of y in any of the equations. Let us use Equation (3)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.396,
            0.24,
            0.599,
            0.275
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_22_equation_5.jpg",
          "content": "x = 3 - 2\\left(\\frac{19}{29}\\right) = \\frac{49}{29}."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.268,
            0.876,
            0.304
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Therefore, the solution of the Equations (1) and (2) is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x = \\frac{49}{29}, y = \\frac{19}{29}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.308,
            0.88,
            0.368
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "To verify if the solution is correct, we substitute "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x = \\frac{49}{29}, y = \\frac{19}{29}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , in Equations (1) and (2) and check if they are satisfied. Try it for yourself."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.385,
            0.4,
            0.409
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think and Reflect"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.421,
            0.852,
            0.473
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What if we had expressed y in terms of x, i.e., take Equation (2) and write "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( 3 - x \\right)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Would we still get the same solution?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.493,
            0.881,
            0.534
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 8:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " If two angles of a triangle are "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x ^ { 0 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and y° with "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y   =   4 x ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and the third angle is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "5 0 ^ { \\circ }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , what are the angles?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.537,
            0.69,
            0.559
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The three angles of the triangle are "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x ^ { 0 } , y ^ { 0 } ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "5 0 ^ { \\circ }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.562,
            0.61,
            0.584
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Using the angle sum property of triangles,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.421,
            0.586,
            0.879,
            0.608
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_22_equation_13.jpg",
          "content": "x^{\\circ} + y^{\\circ} + 50^{\\circ} = 180^{\\circ}\\tag{1}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.613,
            0.214,
            0.632
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Also,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.528,
            0.613,
            0.879,
            0.633
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_22_equation_15.jpg",
          "content": "y = 4 x .\\tag{2}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.636,
            0.609,
            0.659
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Substituting the value of y in Equation (1),"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.664,
            0.233,
            0.683
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "we get"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.424,
            0.661,
            0.607,
            0.732
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_22_equation_18.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}x + 4x + 50 &= 180, \\\\5x &= 130, \\\\x &= 26^{\\circ}.\\end{aligned}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.737,
            0.416,
            0.757
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Therefore, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y = 4x = 104^{\\circ}."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.77,
            0.372,
            0.791
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Elimination Method"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.798,
            0.884,
            0.877
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now let us consider the method of elimination for solving a pair of linear equations. This requires us to eliminate one of the variables x or y. Sometimes it is more convenient than the substitution method. Let us see how this method works."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
